n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <p>Imagine it=E2=80=99s May 2028. Your organization has just taken =
ownership of a brand-new data center, and you=E2=80=99re thrilled at the vo=
lume of workloads it can handle while keeping cloud costs under control. Yo=
u=E2=80=99ve finally figured out and implemented a new hybrid infrastructur=
e strategy. Then your CEO forwards you an article about quantum computing a=
nd asks how it fits into the picture. How will you respond? Will you need t=
o reengineer your systems?</p>
<p>The scenario may still be hypothetical, but it could be all too possible=
 in the coming years. =E2=80=9CBased on vendor road maps, we expect to see =
the first commercially relevant quantum computing use cases in the next 24 =
to 36 months,=E2=80=9D says Scott Buchholz, the US-based quantum computing =
lead for Deloitte Consulting. =E2=80=9CAs companies make infrastructure pla=
ns for growing workloads, they should be thinking about quantum computing a=
nd how it might need to be integrated into evolving high-performance comput=
ing strategies.=E2=80=9D</p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>
<div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pr-0 pl-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-GridColumn--=
phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large aem-GridColumn aem-GridColumn--def=
ault--12">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h2>The eventuality of QPUs</h2>
<p>For many years, enterprises =E2=80=9Cgot most of their horsepower out of=
 mainframes and traditional CPUs,=E2=80=9D says Diana Kearns-Manolatos, tec=
hnology transformation research leader in the Deloitte Center for Integrate=
d Research. Today that=E2=80=99s changing. =E2=80=9COrganizations are makin=
g different choices based on their overall priorities, shifting to increasi=
ngly hybrid strategies with data hosted and algorithms running across publi=
c and private cloud, edge solutions, and private data centers,=E2=80=9D she=
 says. =E2=80=9CData sovereignty, cost, speed, latency, and control all fac=
tor into these hybrid computing strategies.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>While many road maps are focused on achieving mass scale capabilities, o=
rganizations may be missing future interoperability and hybrid computing co=
nnection points if their view is too short-sighted. Sooner than many might =
expect, QPUs=E2=80=94or quantum processing units=E2=80=94could be part of t=
he mix for some enterprises, says Mekena McGrew, the US-based quantum infor=
mation lead for Deloitte Consulting: =E2=80=9CThe preparation needed for en=
terprises to integrate quantum algorithms and the QPUs to process them into=
 their infrastructure road map is considerable.=E2=80=9D</p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>
<div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pr-0 pl-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-GridColumn--=
phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large aem-GridColumn aem-GridColumn--def=
ault--12">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h2>As lasers are to light bulbs</h2>
<p>There are fundamental differences between quantum and classical computer=
s that will require reframing business problems with quantum information sc=
ience principles in mind, Kearns-Manolatos says.</p>
<p>Indeed, <a></a><a href=3D"https://deloitte.wsj.com/cio/the-quantum-era-i=
s-nigh-are-you-ready-14de3ba0">quantum computers</a>&nbsp;are as similar to=
 classical computers =E2=80=9Cas lasers are to light bulbs,=E2=80=9D Buchho=
lz says. =E2=80=9CThey might both emit light, but they are not substitutes =
for one another.=E2=80=9D As a result, =E2=80=9Cthere are some problems tha=
t may be tractable to quantum computers that are not tractable to today=E2=
=80=99s supercomputers.=E2=80=9D</p>

    </div>
</div>

